# X-Touch Mini bridge -- REAPER OSC pattern file
#
# Install to ~/.config/REAPER/OSC/XTouchMini.ReaperOSC (REAPER only scans that
# folder when the dialog opens, so reopen it after copying), then add the
# surface in Preferences -> Control/OSC/web -> Add -> OSC:
#     Mode          Configure device IP+local port
#     Local port    8010            <- the daemon sends here
#     Device IP     127.0.0.1
#     Device port   9010            <- REAPER sends feedback here
#     Pattern       XTouchMini
# "Local port [receive only]" also works; the feedback half is an
# optimisation, not a requirement. See README.md.
# Port 8000 and csurf_0 belong to the ShuttleXpress bridge; leave them alone.
#
# Derived from Default.ReaperOSC, cut down to what the bridge actually uses.
# Everything omitted is feedback REAPER would otherwise send us 30 times a
# second for controls this device does not have.

# The bank has to cover every track you might put a plugin on, because
# /track/<N>/ is an index into THIS BANK, not a REAPER track number.
#
# Measured, with a plugin focused on track 2 while track 2 was selected:
#
#   /track/2/fx/2/fxparam/7/value   ignored
#   /track/1/fx/2/fxparam/7/value   moved it
#
# With DEVICE_TRACK_COUNT 1 the only addressable slot is 1, and
# DEVICE_TRACK_FOLLOWS LAST_TOUCHED points it at the SELECTED track. That is
# right for the fader, which is meant to follow the selection, and wrong for
# the encoders, which drive the focused FX wherever it lives. Select a
# different track and every encoder write either vanishes or -- worse -- lands
# on whatever plugin sits at the same chain position on the selected track.
#
# A bank this wide costs nothing here: the only track feedback declared below
# is the bare /track/... form, which describes the current track alone. Only
# patterns containing @ are expanded per slot. Raise this past 128 if a project
# ever puts a controllable plugin on a higher-numbered track.
DEVICE_TRACK_COUNT 128
DEVICE_SEND_COUNT 0
DEVICE_RECEIVE_COUNT 0
DEVICE_FX_COUNT 1
DEVICE_MARKER_COUNT 0
DEVICE_REGION_COUNT 0

# 128 parameters in one bank, so /fxparam/<K>/value feedback arrives for any
# parameter index up to 127 without the daemon ever having to page the bank.
# Parameters above 127 still get *written* correctly, because writes use the
# explicit /track/@/fx/@/fxparam/@/value form -- they just get no LED feedback.
DEVICE_FX_PARAM_COUNT 128
DEVICE_FX_INST_PARAM_COUNT 128

# The device's notion of "current track" follows REAPER, never the other way
# round: the panel is the only thing allowed to decide what is active. This
# governs the bare /track/... address (the fader) only -- the encoders address
# their track by absolute number and are unaffected by the selection.
REAPER_TRACK_FOLLOWS DEVICE
DEVICE_TRACK_FOLLOWS LAST_TOUCHED
DEVICE_TRACK_BANK_FOLLOWS DEVICE

# FOCUSED, not LAST_TOUCHED: the panel enforces strict focus, and this keeps
# REAPER's feedback pointed at the same plugin the panel is showing.
DEVICE_FX_FOLLOWS FOCUSED

DEVICE_EQ INSERT
DEVICE_ROTARY_CENTER 0

# --- daemon -> REAPER ------------------------------------------------------

# Reserved buttons bound to REAPER actions. The @ captures a named command ID
# such as _RS<sha1>, which is how the registered panel actions are fired.
ACTION t/action/@ s/action/str i/action

# Fader. BOTH forms are declared, and the daemon sends the explicit one.
#
# The bare /track/volume goes to the device's own track, which
# DEVICE_TRACK_FOLLOWS pins to the LAST TOUCHED track "in the REAPER window"
# (REAPER's words). Selecting a track from another control surface does not
# touch it in that sense, so the bare form goes on driving the previous track
# while the panel correctly shows the new one -- the fader appears stuck to a
# track you have already left. Only a mouse click updates it.
#
# The explicit form takes the track number from state.json, which reads the
# real selection every frame however it was made. Same reasoning as the FX
# writes above: the panel is the authority, not REAPER's notion of current.
#
# The @ form means REAPER sends volume feedback for every track in the bank,
# not just one; the daemon keeps only the selected track's and drops the rest.
TRACK_VOLUME n/track/volume n/track/@/volume
TRACK_VOLUME s/track/volume/str
TRACK_NAME s/track/name
TRACK_NUMBER s/track/number/str

# Encoders and mappable buttons. The explicit track/fx/param form is what the
# daemon writes with -- resolved indices come from state.json, so REAPER's own
# idea of what is focused can never disagree with the panel's.
FX_PARAM_VALUE n/track/@/fx/@/fxparam/@/value

# --- REAPER -> daemon (feedback) -------------------------------------------

# Drives the LED rings, and keeps the daemon's shadow values correct when a
# parameter is moved with the mouse instead of the controller.
FX_PARAM_VALUE n/fxparam/@/value
FX_PARAM_VALUE s/fxparam/@/value/str
FX_PARAM_NAME s/fxparam/@/name

# REQUIRED, not decoration. REAPER sends these immediately before the burst of
# parameter values that follows a focus change, and they are the only thing
# that says which plugin the burst is about. Without them the daemon cannot
# tell a burst for the newly focused FX from one for the FX it is still driving
# -- and adopting the wrong one leaves every LED ring showing another plugin's
# parameters. See Engine._feedback_is_for_focused_fx.
FX_NAME s/fx/name
FX_NUMBER s/fx/number/str
